item.text:
Provisional severity class from the 2022 domestic record: recurring to systematic repression with strong state nexus, wide impunity, and selective punishment. I did not score.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian Federation: Authorities impose censorship on media and crush peaceful anti-war protests following invasion of Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur46/5272/2022/en/","claim":"Roskomnadzor effectively ordered media to rely only on official Russian sources about the invasion and threatened sanctions for reporting from other sources.","locator":"lines 67-69","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Amnesty statement published four days after the invasion with direct policy description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediate post-invasion domestic information-control evidence for 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Putin's presidency and the Russian state's censorship apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["The statement reports the official rationale but does not validate it."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Kremlin’s ruthless crackdown stifles independent journalism and anti-war movement","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-03-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/03/russia-kremlins-ruthless-crackdown-stifles-independent-journalism-and-anti-war-movement/","claim":"Amnesty said Russia had blocked independent media, banned Facebook and Twitter, and arbitrarily arrested more than 13,800 people at anti-war rallies since 24 February 2022.","locator":"headline summary and opening bullets","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 field summary with an explicit arrest count and named restrictions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers the first two weeks of the full-scale war and its domestic repression response.","ruler_attribution":"The crackdown was carried out by Russian authorities under Putin.","contrary_evidence":["The arrest figure is a monitoring estimate, not an official government total."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia Criminalizes Independent War Reporting, Anti-War Protests","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/03/07/russia-criminalizes-independent-war-reporting-anti-war-protests","claim":"HRW said Putin signed laws on 4 March 2022 that criminalized independent war reporting and anti-war protest, with penalties of up to 15 years in prison.","locator":"opening summary and update paragraph","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific law-adoption dates and penalties are named in a contemporaneous HRW report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 domestic repression wave.","ruler_attribution":"The laws were signed by Putin and enforced by his state institutions.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed the laws as anti-disinformation measures."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"More than 4,300 detained at anti-war protests in Russia","publisher":"Euronews","publication_date":"2022-03-07","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/03/07/uk-ukraine-crisis-protests","claim":"Reuters reporting republished by Euronews said police detained more than 4,300 people at anti-war protests across Russia and that one protester was shown beaten on the ground by riot police.","locator":"lines 651-661","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters-reported protest tally with concrete police-violence detail.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early March 2022 anti-war protest wave.","ruler_attribution":"The detentions were carried out by Russian police under Putin's government.","contrary_evidence":["Reuters noted it could not independently verify some social-media footage."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Freedom on the Net 2022 Country Report","publisher":"Freedom House","publication_date":"2022-10-31","url":"https://freedomhouse.org/country/russia/freedom-net/2022","claim":"Freedom House reported that Russia blocked more than 5,000 websites after the invasion, expanded state blocking powers, and deepened self-censorship around war, corruption, and LGBT topics.","locator":"lines 83, 398-403, 423-424, 526-533","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Detailed country report with explicit 2022 findings and explanatory text.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Entire 2022 coverage period.","ruler_attribution":"These were state actions under Putin's rule, primarily through Roskomnadzor and allied agencies.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes broad internet access remained widespread, so the issue is control and censorship rather than total shutdown."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Authorities deploy new criminal laws to silence criticism of Russia’s war in Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-09-02","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur46/5988/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said people were already being detained in 2022 for public criticism of the war, and that the new criminal laws were incompatible with freedom of expression.","locator":"lines 67-70","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly contemporaneous with the wartime legal crackdown and explicitly tied to detentions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-summer 2022 repression record.","ruler_attribution":"The laws and detentions were under Putin's authority.","contrary_evidence":["The statement is advocacy-based and does not itself adjudicate every individual case."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russian journalists are being silenced to stifle reporting of protests","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2022/11/russia-journalists-and-independent-monitors-being-silenced-to-stifle-reporting-of-protests-new-report/","claim":"Amnesty said the Russian authorities used severe reprisals, arbitrary arrests, force, detentions, and heavy fines to crush public protest and suppress reporting by journalists and monitors.","locator":"lines 61-66, 71-87","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Focused 2022 report with clear domestic-safety implications and detailed methods of repression.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 domestic protest environment.","ruler_attribution":"The suppression is attributable to Russian state authorities under Putin.","contrary_evidence":["The report also notes the anti-war movement still generated protests despite the crackdown."],"lenses":["3B.5","3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"We publish a report on political prisoners and political repression in Russia in 2022","publisher":"Political prisoners support. Memorial","publication_date":"2023-08-04","url":"https://memopzk.org/en/analytics/we-publish-a-report-on-political-prisoners-and-political-repression-in-russia-in-2022-2/","claim":"Memorial said political prisoners in Russia reached 513 by the end of 2022 and described the year as a turning point for harsher repression.","locator":"lines 73-75","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized political-prisoner monitoring project summarizing its 2022 annual report with explicit counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly about 2022 repression levels.","ruler_attribution":"The political repression occurred under Putin's state system.","contrary_evidence":["The figure is an incomplete list and therefore a conservative count."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Authorities impose censorship on media and crush peaceful anti-war protests following invasion of Ukraine","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/eur46/5272/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty described the censorship order as part of a broader effort that also crushed peaceful anti-war protests and dissent.","locator":"lines 67-69 and report framing","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Same source, distinct passage linking censorship to protest suppression.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Early 2022 domestic climate of repression.","ruler_attribution":"The broader repression was carried out by Russian authorities under Putin.","contrary_evidence":["This is a civil-liberties source, not a criminal court finding."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2023: Russian Federation","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2023-01-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2023/country-chapters/russian-federation","claim":"HRW reported that torture and ill-treatment by law-enforcement officials persisted in 2022, including beatings, electroshock, waterboarding allegations, and punishment cells for Navalny and Yuri Dmitriyev.","locator":"lines 141-150","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual report with specific 2022 incidents, official in time for 2022 conduct assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Covers March to October 2022 domestic policing and prison abuse.","ruler_attribution":"These abuses occurred under Putin's security and prison system.","contrary_evidence":["The report describes specific incidents and allegations; it does not claim all custody abuse was centrally directed in each case."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.6"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2022: Russia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-01-13","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2022/country-chapters/russia","claim":"HRW said authorities regularly let torture, ill-treatment, and suspicious deaths in custody go unpunished, and documented Chechen abductions, anti-LGBT attacks, and failures to investigate domestic violence and police misconduct.","locator":"lines 134-143 and 183-196","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Annual rights report with a broad, well-specified baseline for inherited and continuing abuse.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline and early-2022 continuity.","ruler_attribution":"These are national-level abuses and failures under Putin's rule; some Chechnya conduct is by Kadyrov's local apparatus but with federal responsibility issues raised in the report.","contrary_evidence":["Some abuses are regional or locally executed, so attribution to Putin is indirect rather than always immediate."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.7","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Правозащитные организации обратились к Владимиру Путину по факту похищения родственников критиков главы Чечни","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-01-07","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/ru/latest/research/2022/01/pravozashhitnye-organizaczii-obratilis-k-vladimiru-putinu-po-faktu-pohishheniya-rodstvennikov-kritikov-glavy-chechni/","claim":"A coalition of rights groups told Putin that dozens of relatives of Chechen critics had been abducted, their whereabouts were unknown, and the acts appeared to be enforced disappearances carried out by state-linked forces.","locator":"lines 58-80","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct 2022 letter to Putin with specific victims, alleged methods, and responsibility claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Immediately before the 2022 invasion, showing inherited domestic coercion in Russia's federation.","ruler_attribution":"The letter explicitly assigns federal responsibility to Putin as president and guarantor of rights.","contrary_evidence":["The underlying facts concern Chechnya and some details were still being clarified at the time."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Report 2022: Russia","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-01-13","url":"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2022/country-chapters/russia","claim":"HRW said Russia's government continued anti-LGBT discrimination and failed to investigate threats and attacks against LGBT people, and that domestic violence remained a systemic failure with poor police response and inadequate shelters.","locator":"lines 181-196","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The report ties 2022 domestic protection failures to named vulnerable groups and institutional omissions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Baseline into 2022 and continuing through the year.","ruler_attribution":"These are national-level protection failures under Putin's government, not just private harms.","contrary_evidence":["Some incidents are local and some victims left the country, so the report is a pattern-level source rather than a total tally."],"lenses":["3B.7","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia: Expanded 'Gay Propaganda' Ban Progresses Toward Law","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-11-25","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/11/25/russia-expanded-gay-propaganda-ban-progresses-toward-law","claim":"HRW said the State Duma backed legislation that would further restrict LGBT expression, including positive or neutral information about LGBT people, with hefty fines and broader stigmatization.","locator":"lines 604-618","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear late-2022 legislative analysis with legal and rights implications spelled out.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late 2022 domestic vulnerability and incitement environment.","ruler_attribution":"This legislative trajectory occurred under Putin and his ruling coalition.","contrary_evidence":["Lawmakers said they were defending morality and 'traditional values'."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Russia's parliament passes law banning 'LGBT propaganda' among adults","publisher":"OPENLY","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://www.openlynews.com/i/?id=4525aecd-135a-43d1-b3a2-8cfcddbf5407","claim":"Reuters reported that Russia expanded its LGBT-propaganda ban to all ages, with fines and possible arrest for foreigners, in a move critics said was meant to intimidate and oppress sexual minorities.","locator":"lines 33-47","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-reported legislative coverage with direct details on scope and penalties.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exactly within 2022 and directly relevant to vulnerable-group protection.","ruler_attribution":"The law was being pushed and would be signed under Putin's authority.","contrary_evidence":["The government framed it as a morality and child-protection measure."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.7","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"‘The purification of society will only strengthen the country’ Meduza’s brief retelling of Putin’s darkest speech yet","publisher":"Meduza","publication_date":"2022-03-18","url":"https://meduza.io/en/feature/2022/03/18/the-purification-of-society-will-only-strengthen-the-country","claim":"Meduza reported that Putin publicly denounced a 'fifth column' of 'traitors' and praised 'purification of society,' while claiming Russia was forced into war and that troops were avoiding civilian casualties.","locator":"lines 14-23","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Directly reports a specific Putin speech with language relevant to incitement and fear climate.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"March 2022 domestic-rhetoric escalation during wartime.","ruler_attribution":"The rhetoric is directly attributable to Putin.","contrary_evidence":["The article is a retelling rather than a Kremlin transcript."],"lenses":["3B.3","3B.6","3B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factbox: Memorial, chronicler of Soviet and recent crimes, banned by Putin","publisher":"Euronews","publication_date":"2022-10-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/10/08/nobel-prize-peace-memorial","claim":"Reuters-reported coverage said Russia ordered Memorial dissolved in 2021, capping a year of crackdowns on Kremlin critics and removing a key civil-society watchdog from the domestic accountability landscape.","locator":"lines 664-677","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters factbox hosted by Euronews with direct explanation of Memorial's dissolution and function.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited repression baseline relevant to 2022 accountability and remedy.","ruler_attribution":"The dissolution occurred under Putin's rule and affected domestic oversight capacity.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a factbox about Memorial, not a formal legal finding about each prosecution it handled."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Москва | Human Rights Center MEMORIAL","publisher":"Human Rights Center MEMORIAL","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://memohrc.org/en/regions/moskva","claim":"Memorial's Moscow region page listed ongoing 2022 political-prisoner cases, including Navalny associates, journalists, and other defendants, showing continued tracking of politically motivated prosecutions.","locator":"lines 98-145 and 151-205","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct case-listing page with dated 2022 entries, though it is a rolling case docket rather than a single report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Spring 2022 active repression environment.","ruler_attribution":"These prosecutions occurred under Putin's state apparatus.","contrary_evidence":["It is a case registry, so the page reflects Memorial's selection and updating rather than a closed annual count."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Положение политических заключенных в России","publisher":"Memorial Center","publication_date":"2023-04-05","url":"https://memorialcenter.org/analytics/polozhenie-politicheskih-zaklyuchennyh-v-rf","claim":"Memorial's joint submission reported that the general political-prisoner list had grown sharply, from 46 in 2018 to 141 by 5 April 2023, with a separate religious list of 410, illustrating the scale of repression carried through 2022 and beyond.","locator":"opening section on list statistics","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Specialized human-rights dossier with explicit list counts, though the page is a 2023 submission about a longer trend.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Trendline that brackets the 2022 repression environment.","ruler_attribution":"The growth of political imprisonment occurred under Putin's rule.","contrary_evidence":["The page is a later submission and includes a broader period than just 2022."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"ОВД-Инфо","publisher":"ОВД-Инфо","publication_date":"2026-07-21","url":"https://ovd.info/","claim":"OVD-Info describes Russia as a place where politically motivated detentions, interrogations, searches, and arrests occur regularly and says it collects and publishes repression data for the public.","locator":"lines 23-25 and 43-47, 87-94","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Current project homepage describing mission and scale, useful for mapping the domestic repression information environment but not a year-specific report.","final_evidence_use":"discovery_only","period_fit":"General repression-context source rather than a 2022 incident report.","ruler_attribution":"The project describes repression under the Russian state system led by Putin.","contrary_evidence":["This is a live organizational homepage, so it is weaker than a dated annual report for year-specific claims."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.8","3B.10"]}

Search ledger:
- Candidates found: about 60
- Documents opened or partially opened: 32
- Duplicate families removed: about 18
- Irrelevant / out-of-period / weak context-only items dropped: about 12
- Inaccessible or timeout-blocked items: about 8
- Accepted distinct URLs this wave: 17
- Distinct domains represented in accepted evidence this wave: 10

Material gaps:
- I still need a cleaner 2022 annual source for exact anti-war arrest totals, political-prisoner totals, and regional breakdowns, ideally from OVD-Info or Memorial, rather than later summary pages.
- The Chechnya record would benefit from a 2022 or late-2022 source from an official inquiry, UN mechanism, or a tightly dated investigative report on kidnappings and disappearances.
- I have strong evidence for protest repression, censorship, torture, LGBT discrimination, and domestic-violence neglect, but less on positive restraint or effective remedy, which itself is an important signal for this chapter.
- If you want a second discovery wave, the next best queries are: 2022 Russian court records on anti-war "fake news" cases, 2022 regional domestic-violence reports, and 2022 prison-oversight or ombudsman material.
